     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Checked against developer.android.com "Dependency injection with Hilt" (component lifetimes; "Injector for":
     ViewModelComponent → ViewModel; ActivityRetainedComponent lives across configuration changes) on 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A shopping cart lives in a class annotated <code>@ActivityScoped</code>. It works in every demo, until a tester rotates the phone and the cart empties. The quick fix moves it to <code>@Singleton</code>; now the cart survives rotation, but also survives logout, and the next user of a shared tablet sees the previous user's items.</p>

Both fixes chose a scope by trial and error. In Hilt, a scope is a promise about **which container holds the instance**, and each container is created and destroyed with a specific Android lifetime. This chapter maps every standard component to that lifetime, shows how a request finds its binding, and covers the escape hatches for code Hilt does not create.

<!-- @after The Hierarchy (Visualized) :: animation -->
### Resolving a request walks up, never down

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Where Hilt looks for each binding</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node" data-el="single" data-art="graph" data-art-role="runtime"><strong><code>SingletonComponent</code></strong><small><code>ImageLoader</code> (<code>@Singleton</code>)</small></div><div class="node" data-el="retained" data-art="graph" data-art-role="runtime"><strong><code>ActivityRetainedComponent</code></strong></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="vm" data-art="graph" data-art-role="runtime"><strong><code>ViewModelComponent</code></strong><small><code>CartViewModel</code></small></div><div class="node" data-el="act" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong><code>ActivityComponent</code></strong><small><code>ToolbarController</code> (<code>@ActivityScoped</code>)</small></div></div><div class="node" data-el="frag" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong><code>FragmentComponent</code></strong><small><code>ProductListFragment</code></small></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="result" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Result</strong><small data-text="resultV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"The graph","caption":"Three bindings live in three components. The fragment's component sits under the activity component, which sits under the retained and singleton components.","add":["single","retained","vm","act","frag","result"],"state":{},"text":{"resultV":"nothing requested yet"}},
{"label":"Fragment needs ImageLoader","caption":"The fragment's component does not provide it, so Hilt looks at its ancestors: activity, retained, singleton. Found at the top.","state":{"frag":"active","act":"ghost","retained":"ghost","single":"done"},"text":{"resultV":"✓ resolved in SingletonComponent"}},
{"label":"Fragment needs ToolbarController","caption":"One step up, the activity component provides it. The fragment gets the activity's instance, recreated on rotation along with it.","state":{"single":"","retained":"","act":"done"},"text":{"resultV":"✓ resolved in ActivityComponent"}},
{"label":"ViewModel needs ToolbarController","caption":"The ViewModel component's ancestors are the retained and singleton components. The activity component is a sibling branch, so the binding is unreachable and the build fails.","state":{"frag":"","act":"error","vm":"active","retained":"ghost","single":"ghost"},"text":{"resultV":"✗ compile error (incompatible scope)"}},
{"label":"Why that is good","caption":"A ViewModel outlives each activity instance. If Hilt let it hold an activity-scoped object, it would keep a destroyed activity alive. The compile error is the leak, caught early.","state":{"act":"warn","vm":"done"},"text":{"resultV":"pass data in, or re-scope the dependency"}}
]</script><figcaption>Choose the smallest component whose lifetime covers every consumer, and Hilt checks the rest at build time.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after EntryPoints — Escape Hatch for Non-Hilt Classes :: visual -->
### An entry point is a typed door into a component

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong><code>ContentProvider</code></strong><small>created by the system before Hilt can inject it</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong><code>@EntryPoint</code> interface</strong><small>installed in the component that holds the binding</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong><code>EntryPointAccessors.fromApplication()</code></strong><small>returns that component as the interface</small></div></div><figcaption>The interface is generated into the component at compile time, so the lookup is a cast, not reflection.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Pitfalls :: check -->
**Which component?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>A cart that must survive rotation but reset when the user finishes the activity.</summary>

`@ActivityRetainedScoped`, or state in the screen's ViewModel. `@ActivityScoped` resets on rotation; `@Singleton` outlives the user's session.
</details>

<details><summary>Per-user state that lives from login to logout across several activities.</summary>

A custom component (`@DefineComponent(parent = SingletonComponent::class)` with its own scope), built on login and released on logout.
</details>

<details><summary>A helper that needs the current activity's theme to inflate a dialog.</summary>

`ActivityComponent` with `@ActivityContext`. It is recreated with the activity, which is exactly what its context requires.
</details>
